EC2 3-Server SSH Setup

We have 3 EC2 instances:

Server-1
Server-2
Server-3

Step 1 — Set Hostname

Run these commands on each server.

On Server-1

sudo hostnamectl set-hostname server1
exec bash

On Server-2

sudo hostnamectl set-hostname server2
exec bash

On Server-3

sudo hostnamectl set-hostname server3
exec bash

Check:

hostname


Step 2 — Add Private IP and Hostnames


First, get private IP of each server:

ip a

or 

hostname -I

For Example:

Server-1 → 10.0.0.10
Server-2 → 10.0.0.11
Server-3 → 10.0.0.12

Now on all three servers, open hosts file:

sudo vi /etc/hosts

Add:

10.0.0.10   server1
10.0.0.11   server2
10.0.0.12   server3

:wq --- Save and exit.


Step 3 — Generate SSH Key on All Servers

Run this on:

Server-1
Server-2
Server-3

ssh-keygen

Press Enter for all prompts.

Get public key on each server:

cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub

Copy:

Server-1 public key
Server-2 public key
Server-3 public key


Step 4 — Exchange SSH Keys

Each server will add the other two servers’ keys into its authorized_keys file.

On Server-1

vi ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Paste:

Server-2 public key
Server-3 public key

:wq --- Save & exit.

chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys


On Server-2

vi ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Paste:

Server-1 public key
Server-3 public key

:wq --- Save & exit.

chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

On Server-3

vi ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Paste:

Server-1 public key
Server-2 public key

:wq --- Save & exit.

chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys



Step 5 — Test SSH

From any server:

ssh server1

The first time, you will see:

Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no)?

Type: yes

and press Enter.

Now test the others:

ssh server2
ssh server3

Again, type yes if asked.

If no password is requested and login works,
your SSH setup is successful ✅
